WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.977
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level four.

2
00:00:06.277 --> 00:00:09.640
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:15.890
Unit six: Global Trade Agreements. Today you will comment on a whole clause with sentence relatives.

4
00:00:16.190 --> 00:00:18.761
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:23.026
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.883 --> 00:00:25.082
Free trade agreement.

7
00:00:26.083 --> 00:00:31.403
Free trade agreement means a deal that removes taxes and barriers on goods between countries.

8
00:00:32.017 --> 00:00:34.782
The free trade agreement covered twelve countries.

9
00:00:35.383 --> 00:00:38.488
Your turn. Say: Free trade agreement.

10
00:00:41.333 --> 00:00:42.776
Free trade agreement.

11
00:00:43.517 --> 00:00:44.541
Trade bloc.

12
00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:49.883
Trade bloc means a group of countries with special trading rules among themselves.

13
00:00:50.483 --> 00:00:52.982
The trade bloc welcomed three new members.

14
00:00:53.583 --> 00:00:56.236
Your turn. Say: Trade bloc.

15
00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:00.148
Trade bloc.

16
00:01:00.883 --> 00:01:02.093
Tariff reduction.

17
00:01:03.083 --> 00:01:07.080
Tariff reduction means lowering the tax charged on imported goods.

18
00:01:07.683 --> 00:01:09.973
Tariff reduction made cars cheaper.

19
00:01:10.583 --> 00:01:13.723
Your turn. Say: Tariff reduction.

20
00:01:16.567 --> 00:01:17.626
Tariff reduction.

21
00:01:18.383 --> 00:01:20.104
Economic partnership.

22
00:01:21.117 --> 00:01:26.030
Economic partnership means a close agreement on trade and investment between countries.

23
00:01:26.617 --> 00:01:29.928
The economic partnership created thousands of jobs.

24
00:01:30.517 --> 00:01:33.947
Your turn. Say: Economic partnership.

25
00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:38.544
Economic partnership.

26
00:01:39.283 --> 00:01:40.668
Market access.

27
00:01:41.683 --> 00:01:46.086
Market access means the ability of a firm to sell its goods in another country.

28
00:01:46.683 --> 00:01:49.600
Market access allowed farmers to export fruit.

29
00:01:50.217 --> 00:01:53.542
Your turn. Say: Market access.

30
00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:57.865
Market access.

31
00:01:58.617 --> 00:01:59.838
Trade dispute.

32
00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:05.381
Trade dispute means a disagreement between countries about trade rules or taxes.

33
00:02:05.983 --> 00:02:08.819
The trade dispute reached an international court.

34
00:02:09.417 --> 00:02:12.603
Your turn. Say: Trade dispute.

35
00:02:15.467 --> 00:02:16.595
Trade dispute.

36
00:02:17.350 --> 00:02:18.792
Global supply chain.

37
00:02:19.783 --> 00:02:24.337
Global supply chain means the worldwide network that produces and delivers goods.

38
00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:28.354
The global supply chain links factories on every continent.

39
00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:32.821
Your turn. Say: Global supply chain.

40
00:02:35.667 --> 00:02:37.399
Global supply chain.

41
00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:39.198
Protectionism.

42
00:02:40.183 --> 00:02:44.876
Protectionism means the policy of shielding home industries from foreign competition.

43
00:02:45.483 --> 00:02:48.319
Protectionism can raise prices for shoppers.

44
00:02:48.917 --> 00:02:51.720
Your turn. Say: Protectionism.

45
00:02:54.567 --> 00:02:55.742
Protectionism.

46
00:02:56.533 --> 00:03:00.370
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:03:01.217 --> 00:03:07.422
Visal, an article says 'The tariffs were removed, which boosted exports.' What does 'which' refer to?

48
00:03:08.517 --> 00:03:18.024
It refers to the whole clause before it, not one noun. It is a sentence relative clause. 'Which' means 'and this fact'. It always follows a comma.

49
00:03:19.117 --> 00:03:20.547
Can I use 'that' instead?

50
00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:30.354
No. Never use 'that' after a comma. Say 'The agreement collapsed, which surprised investors', not '..., that surprised investors'.

51
00:03:31.450 --> 00:03:33.217
Which verbs usually follow?

52
00:03:34.317 --> 00:03:47.061
Often result or opinion verbs: which caused, which meant, which is why. 'Tariff reduction widened market access, which meant cheaper goods for consumers.'

53
00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:50.083
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:50.950 --> 00:03:54.796
Grammar focus: Sentence relative: , which plus comment.

55
00:03:55.096 --> 00:03:56.294
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:56.983 --> 00:04:00.713
The tariffs were removed, which boosted exports across the bloc.

57
00:04:01.617 --> 00:04:05.706
The trade dispute dragged on for years, which damaged the global supply chain.

58
00:04:06.617 --> 00:04:10.810
Tariff reduction widened market access, which meant cheaper goods for consumers.

59
00:04:11.767 --> 00:04:19.829
After a comma, which can refer to a whole clause: The tariffs were removed, which boosted exports. Never use that in this position.

60
00:04:20.583 --> 00:04:25.116
Be careful! Don't say: He left early, that surprised us.

61
00:04:27.067 --> 00:04:30.065
Say: He left early, which surprised us.

62
00:04:30.365 --> 00:04:32.515
After a comma, use 'which', not 'that'.

63
00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:36.034
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:36.833 --> 00:04:38.937
Winners and Losers of Free Trade.

65
00:04:39.237 --> 00:04:44.906
A free trade agreement removes barriers between countries, which encourages firms to sell abroad.

66
00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:50.779
Tariff reduction gives better market access, which usually lowers prices for shoppers.

67
00:04:51.767 --> 00:04:56.947
Within a trade bloc, partners enjoy an economic partnership, which can deepen political ties.

68
00:04:57.933 --> 00:05:03.091
Yet protectionism may return after a trade dispute, which slows the global supply chain.

69
00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:09.002
Governments must therefore weigh cheaper goods against jobs in industries that lose protection.

70
00:05:10.100 --> 00:05:14.009
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:14.850 --> 00:05:18.231
The factory closed, blank left many workers jobless.

72
00:05:18.531 --> 00:05:21.546
A, that. B, which. C, what.

73
00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:26.763
The answer is B: which.

74
00:05:27.917 --> 00:05:30.311
Taxes on imported goods are called blank.

75
00:05:30.611 --> 00:05:34.497
A, tariffs. B, summits. C, visas.

76
00:05:38.033 --> 00:05:40.172
The answer is A: tariffs.

77
00:05:41.317 --> 00:05:42.794
Which sentence is correct?

78
00:05:43.094 --> 00:05:49.928
A, He resigned, that surprised us.. B, He resigned, which surprised us.. C, He resigned which, surprised us..

79
00:05:53.467 --> 00:05:56.465
The answer is B: He resigned, which surprised us..

80
00:05:57.617 --> 00:06:01.741
A policy of shielding home industry from foreign competition is blank.

81
00:06:02.041 --> 00:06:05.950
A, protectionism. B, integration. C, diplomacy.

82
00:06:09.500 --> 00:06:11.790
The answer is A: protectionism.

83
00:06:12.950 --> 00:06:16.691
According to the reading, what does tariff reduction usually lower?

84
00:06:16.991 --> 00:06:20.691
A, prices. B, wages. C, borders.

85
00:06:24.233 --> 00:06:26.488
The answer is A: prices.

86
00:06:27.700 --> 00:06:30.863
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:31.717 --> 00:06:35.226
What are the advantages and disadvantages of free trade agreements?

88
00:06:44.017 --> 00:06:45.505
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:45.805 --> 00:07:10.450
Free trade agreements lower prices, widen market access and help exporters grow, which creates jobs in competitive industries. However, cheaper imports can hurt local industries that cannot compete, and a trade dispute can damage relations between partners. Governments need to support workers who lose out so that the benefits of trade are shared fairly.

90
00:07:10.750 --> 00:07:12.633
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:13.517 --> 00:07:18.921
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and sentence relative: , which plus comment.

92
00:07:19.221 --> 00:07:24.810
Today's value is global economic literacy. Understand how money and trade connect the world.

93
00:07:25.110 --> 00:07:27.403
See you in Unit seven. Goodbye!

94
00:07:27.703 --> 00:07:30.158
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

